Power Set: Print all the possible subsequences of the String 

Problem Statement:
Given the string find all the possible subsequences of the string.

What are subsequences?
A subsequence is a sequence derived from another sequence by deleting some or no elements 
without changing the order of the remaining elements.

Eg1:
Input: str = "abc"
Output: [a, ab, abc, ac, b, bc, c]
The given string has 2^3 - 1 = 7 non-empty subsequences (or 8 if including the empty string "")

Eg2: 
Input: str = "aa"
Output: [a, a, aa]
The given string has 3 non-empty subsequences.

Approach 1: Bit Manipulation
Algo:
Every character in the string has 2 choices: either it is included in the subsequence or excluded.
For a string of length n, there are exactly 2^n possible subsequences.
We can map these choices to binary numbers from 0 to 2^n-1
If the ith bit of the number is set to 1 we include the ith character of the string
if its 0 we exculde it 

class Solution {
public: 
    //Function to return all subsequences of string s 
    vector<string> getSubsequences(string s) {
        int n = s.size();

        //Total Subsequences = 2^n
        int total = 1 << n;
        vector<string> subsequences;

        //Iterate over all bit masks from 0 to 2^n-1
        for (int mask = 0; mask < total; mask++) {
            string subseq = "";

            //Check each bit position in mask 
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                //If i-th bit of mask is set, include s[i]
                if (mask & (1 << i)) {
                    subseq += s[i];
                }
            }

            //Only store non-empty subsequences (or store all based on need)
            if (subseq.length() > 0) {
                subsequences.push_back(subseq);
            }
        }
        return subsequences;
    }
};

TC: O(2^n * n) 
We loop 2^n times (total number of subsets)
For each subset we loop n times to check the individual bits 

SC: O(1)
Aux space is constant
If we consider the array to store all subsequences, it takes O(2^n * n) space.


Approach 2: Recursion / Backtracking (Pick and Don't Pick)
This method uses a decision tree.
We start from the first character and make a choice.
Do I include this character in my current subsequence or not?
We explore the include path by adding the characters and recursing to the next index
We explore the exclude path by skipping the characters and recursing
Once we hit the end of the string we have built a complete subsequence.

class Solution {
public:
    //Helper recursive function to generate subsequences 
    void helper(string &s, int index, string current, vector<string>& result) {
        //Base case: If index reaches string length, add current subsequence 
        if (index == s.size()) {
            if (current.length() > 0) { //Omit if you want the empty string too
                result.push_back(current);
            }
            return;
        }

        //Choice 1: Include the current character 
        helper(s, index + 1, current + s[index], result);

        //Choice 2: Exclude the current character 
        helper(s, index + 1, current, result);
    }

    vector<string> getSubsequence(string s) {
        vector<string> result;
        helper(s, 0, "", result);
        return result;
    }
};

int main() {
    string = "abc";
    Solution sol;
    vector<string> subsequences = sol.getSubsequences(s);

    for (string subseq : subsequences) {
        cout << subseq << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
    return 0;
}

TC: O(2^n) 
At each character we branch out into 2 recursive calls (Pick and Don't Pick)
We generate 2^n nodes in the recursion tree.

SC: O(n)
Aux space
The recursion call stack reaches maximum depth n 

